  <reporting-org ref="XM-DAC-41302" type="40">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">International Labour Organization (ILO)</narrative>
  </reporting-org>
  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Policies and Programmes on HIV/AIDS world of work improved and implemented</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The ILO is actively working to improve policies and programs concerning HIV/AIDS in Uganda&#x2019;s workplaces, addressing the pervasive issues of stigma, discrimination, and violence against individuals affected by HIV/AIDS. This initiative aims to create inclusive, safe, and respectful work environments that promote gender equality, human rights, and the dignity of all workers. The focus is on enhancing the capacity of employers, workers, and government entities to implement effective measures against HIV-related stigma and discrimination.

Beneficiaries of this program include persons living with HIV/AIDS, women and girls particularly vulnerable to violence and infection, persons with disabilities, and other marginalized groups. The anticipated outcomes include training 50 master trainers to spearhead initiatives across various sectors, facilitating the development of appropriate workplace measures, and disseminating key resources such as the Employers&#x2019; Guide on combating HIV-related stigma and discrimination. 

Collaboration will occur among ILO, national employers&#x2019; organizations, labor unions, and civil society groups to promote awareness and implement collective actions in line with ILO Convention No. 190 on Violence and Harassment. By strengthening the advocacy and capacity of all stakeholders, the CPO reaffirms Uganda&apos;s commitment to uphold labor rights while addressing the socio-economic impact of HIV/AIDS in the workplace. These endeavors will significantly contribute to achieving Sustainable Development Goals 5, 8, and 10, by fostering sustained, inclusive growth and equal opportunities for all workers.</narrative>
  </description>
